{ignore}
<p><code>{palette}</code> shows the colours of a palette as a row of swatches, each with its hex
value written on it in black or white, whichever reads better. A palette is a file of
<code>pad/subs/palettes/types/</code> - its name, its type and its colours - and the files there
are the palettes there are; the subsystem is <code>pad/subs/palettes/</code>.</p>

<p>Four types. The sequential ones run from low to high: viridis, magma, plasma, inferno and
cividis from matplotlib's published 256-colour tables, perceptually uniform, readable in grey and
by colour-blind eyes, and ColorBrewer's blues, greens, oranges and greys. The diverging ones meet
in a light middle - ColorBrewer's rdbu, brbg and piyg. The cyclic one goes round, for an angle, a
direction, the hour or the month: Crameri's romaO, whose last colour is next to its first, so
twelve steps of it are twelve colours, none twice. The categorical ones tell things apart:
okabe-ito and Paul Tol's bright, muted and vibrant, made for every kind of colour vision,
ColorBrewer's pastel and PAD's own earth.</p>

<p>The same palettes colour a chart: <code>palette='viridis'</code> on any kind of
<code>{chart}</code> puts the palette's colours on its series - a scale spread over the series the
chart has, so three series are its two ends and its middle - and on the heatmap's ramp, as custom
properties on the svg. A chart drawn in the browser (<code>client</code>) gets the same colours.
<code>padPalette ( 'viridis', 7 )</code> answers them to a page's PHP.</p>

<p>The row is a list - <code>role="list"</code>, a swatch an item - so a screen reader counts
the colours and reads their values.</p>
{/ignore}
